; Depth 1: each head's tail-set is ∅ or {ε}, so `x <| prefixes` keeps ε iff ε ∈ prefixes, and keeps the path `h` iff ε ∈ prefixes or `h` ∈ prefixes.  The transfer: ε by `and`; a head survives only if both sides may have it, with the key set = tracked(prefixes) when x is open and closed on either side closing the result; others.lo = 0 (a prefix set DELETES); others.hi = min; otherTail weakened.  Theorem: A ∈ γ(x) ∧ B ∈ γ(p) ⇒ A<|B ∈ γ(x <|# p).  SCOPE: only the code's GENERAL branch, i.e. `prefixes.eps = No`.  The other branches are guarded earlier and are separately trivial — a MUST-ε prefix set returns x unchanged, a ⊤ or head-empty-open prefix set returns weaken(x), and a MAY-ε prefix set returns the union of weaken(x) with this branch, all sound given this one and the ε rules.  z3 REFUTED the unguarded version: with ε ∈ B the surviving-head count is bounded by x's untracked count alone, not by min(x, prefixes) — which is exactly why the code must branch on may-ε first.
